year><publisher-name>New York: Springer</publisher-name>Corrected 2nd printing edn,</citation></ref><ref id="bib27"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Li</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Zhu</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Yousef</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Nebozhyn</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Showe</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Showe</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Xuan</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Clarke</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>VISDA: an open-source caBIG&#x02122; analytical tool for data clustering and beyond</article-title><source>Bioinformatics</source><year>2007</year><volume>23</volume><fpage>2024</fpage><lpage>2027</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'Bioinformatics||23|2024||bib27|'--><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17540678</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib28"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>Z</given-names></name><name><surname>Wang</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Xuan</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Dong</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Bakay</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Feng</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Clarke</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name><name><surname>Hoffman</surname><given-names>EP</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Optimized multilayer perceptrons for molecular classification and diagnosis using genomic data</article-title><source>Bioinformatics</source><year>2006</year><volume>22</volume><fpage>755</fpage><lpage>761</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'Bioinformatics||22|755||bib28|'--><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16403791</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib29"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Xing</surname><given-names>EP</given-names></name><name><surname>Karp</surname><given-names>RM</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>CLIFF: clustering of high-dimensional microarray data via iterative feature filtering using normalized cuts</article-title><source>Bioinformatics</source><year>2001</year><volume>17</volume><fpage>S306</fpage><lpage>S315</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'Bioinformatics||17|S306||bib29|'--><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11473022</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib30"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yeung</surname><given-names>KY</given-names></name><name><surname>Haynor</surname><given-names>DR</given-names></name><name><surname>Ruzzo</surname><given-names>WL</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Validating clustering for gene expression data</article-title><source>Bioinformatics</source><year>2001</year><volume>17</volume><fpage>309</fpage><lpage>318</lpage><!--PubMed citation query: 'Bioinformatics||17|309||bib30|'--><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11301299</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>A demonstration of the bias/variance dilemma in predictive classification. Specifically, the error of model fitting can be decomposed into two components, bias (approximation error) and variance (estimation error). Added dimensions can degrade the prediction performance if the sample size is small relative to the dimensionality. For a fixed sample size in the high-dimensional data space, there is a tradeoff between the decreased approximation error and the increased estimation error.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6604207f1"/></fig><fig id="fig2"><label>Figure 2</label><caption><p>An example of XOR/chessboard-like joint effects. Although the classes consist of disjoint clusters, each variable has completely overlapping class conditional densities, that is, no marginal effect. In contrast, working together, the two variables provide good class separability.</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6604207f2"/></fig><fig id="fig3"><label>Figure 3</label><caption><p>An example of coarse-to-fine top-down divisive unsupervised clustering using VISDA. (<bold>A</bold>) Multiple complementary visualisation subspaces derived from different data structure preserving projection principles. (<bold>B</bold>) Tree of phenotype with embedded model selection function, where MO refers to the model order (number of clusters) and DL refers to the description length (model complexity as a function of cluster number).</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="eps" xlink:href="6604207f3"/></fig></floats-wrap></article>